.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt PIDFD_OPEN 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pidfd_open ,
.Nm pidfd_send_signal ,
.Nm pidfd_getfd
.Nd descriptors that name a process
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/pidfd.h
.Ft int
.Fn pidfd_open "pid_t pid" "unsigned int flags"
.Ft int
.Fn pidfd_send_signal "int pidfd" "int sig" "siginfo_t *info" "unsigned int flags"
.Ft int
.Fn pidfd_getfd "int pidfd" "int targetfd" "unsigned int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A pidfd is a descriptor that names a process.
Unlike a process ID, it never comes to name another process: once its own
has been reaped and its number given to a new one, the pidfd names none.
.Pp
.Fn pidfd_open
returns a new close-on-exec pidfd of the process
.Fa pid
leads, which may have ended and not yet been reaped.
.Fa flags
is 0 or
.Dv PIDFD_NONBLOCK ,
which makes the descriptor non-blocking.
.Xr fork 2 Ns 's
.Dv CLONE_PIDFD
makes one of the child as it is created.
.Pp
.Xr poll 2
and
.Xr epoll 2
report a pidfd readable once every thread of its process has ended, and
hung up as well once the process has been reaped.
Both events wake a poller already waiting on the pidfd.
.Xr waitid 2
with
.Dv P_PIDFD
waits for the process, when it is a child of the caller's.
A
.Xr read 2
or
.Xr write 2
of a pidfd fails with
.Er EINVAL .
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /fd
names it
.Dq anon_inode:[pidfd] .
.Pp
.Fn pidfd_send_signal
sends
.Fa sig
to the process, as
.Xr kill 2
sends it to a process ID, under the same permission.
A null
.Fa info
gives the receiver
.Xr kill 2 Ns 's
siginfo; otherwise
.Fa info
is the siginfo, whose
.Va si_signo
must be
.Fa sig ,
and whose code a thread may give a process it does not lead only when it
is negative and not
.Dv SI_TKILL ,
as
.Xr rt_sigqueueinfo 2
has it; a native caller's siginfo is refused as there when its code's
layout is unknown and it has bytes past the kept fields.
The kernel copies the whole siginfo before resolving the process, so a
page fault during the copy cannot leave a stale target.
Signal 0 checks the permission and sends nothing, and a signal to a process
whose threads have all ended is discarded.
.Fa flags
must be 0.
.Pp
.Fn pidfd_getfd
returns a close-on-exec copy, in the caller's table, of the process's
descriptor
.Fa targetfd ,
which shares its open file description, as
.Xr dup 2
makes a copy.
The process must be the caller's own, or the caller must be one that may
attach to it with
.Xr ptrace 2 :
privileged, or with a real user ID and a real group ID equal to each of
the process's three, while the process is dumpable and not privileged.
.Fa flags
must be 0.
.Ss System call interface
The calls are numbered the same on i386 and x86-64:
.Nm pidfd_send_signal
424,
.Nm pidfd_open
434 and
.Nm pidfd_getfd
438.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn pidfd_open
and
.Fn pidfd_getfd
return the new descriptor and
.Fn pidfd_send_signal
returns 0; each returns -1 with
.Va errno
set on failure.
The kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/musl/pidfdmusl.c
opens, polls, signals and waits on pidfds, takes a pipe through
.Fn pidfd_getfd ,
and checks the refusals.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EMFILE]"
.It Bq Er E2BIG
.Fa info
has a code of a layout Linux does not know and a byte past the kept
fields that is not zero, as
.Xr rt_sigqueueinfo 2
describes.
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa pidfd
is not an open pidfd, or
.Fa targetfd
is not open in the process, which has none once it has ended.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Fa info
cannot be read.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa pid
is not above 0 or names a thread that does not lead its process;
.Fa flags
has a bit not described above;
.Fa sig
is out of range or is not the
.Va si_signo
of
.Fa info ;
or the process is outside the caller's PID namespace and those below it.
.It Bq Er EMFILE
The caller has no free descriptor below its limit.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The kernel had no memory for the pidfd, a larger descriptor table or an
open file description.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The caller may not signal the process, give it that siginfo code, or take
its descriptors.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
No process has the ID
.Fa pid ,
or the pidfd's process has been reaped.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr kill 2 ,
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr wait 2
